WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.360
Film Courage: Have you ever made a movie for
more than $250,000?

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:04.980
John Paul Rice, Producer: No.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:10.580
Film Courage: And have people said “There
is just no way you are going to do this then.”

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.840
John: Oh yeah!

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:19.590
I talk to people all the time who are experienced
who micro budget to them is half a million,

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:24.320
quarter of a million is kind of like that’s
the lowest they will go and they just can’t

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:25.750
conceive of it.&nbsp;

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:32.210
I mean if I priced out my movies for what
they would cost under normal circumstances

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:37.380
I would say yeah I did a quarter of a million
- half a million every time.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:45.630
But we just plan smart and we shoot the scenes
that we need to shoot and we work within our

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:51.950
limitations and embrace them.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:56.450
I can’t tell you how many times I don’t
think I’ve had a film shoot (even with pickups)

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:00.309
go past 16 days.

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:06.229
Most of it, I mean our last film we shot principal
in I think it was ten or eleven days and then

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:09.290
did two days of pickups.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:15.009
We just were a very efficient group, we didn’t
work over 14 hours that I can recall.

00:01:15.009 --> 00:01:20.270
If we did it may have been one day, most of
it is 12 (12 to 13).

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:23.380
Everybody gets fed.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:24.389
You just roll with it.

00:01:24.389 --> 00:01:29.009
But I would love to, I would love to have
a quarter of a million dollars.&nbsp;

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:34.020
Film Courage: Well you can probably now when
you read a script, when you say what is more

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.719
important or you say what do you do first
the script or the budget you can probably

00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:41.060
see red flags like “Oohhh, this is going
to really cost.”

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.060
Or “Oh!

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.060
This is great, it’s going to really keep
it down.”

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:46.539
John: Yes and that’s a conversation with
the writer you have to talk to them and say

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:53.710
hey do we want to…are you open to the idea
of changing this given the resources we have

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:58.960
financially because we kind of did an analysis
(a general over analysis).

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:04.420
It’s going to take this many number of days
to shoot so it’s got fixed costs.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:09.549
Daily rates for everybody, putting people
up and then we’ve got whatever other things

00:02:09.549 --> 00:02:13.599
we have to do, how many actors we have to
bring in (that kind of thing) and you should

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:20.390
fight for a compromise but it’s one that
is a shared vision as opposed to “You’re

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:21.709
right” and “They’re wrong.”

00:02:21.709 --> 00:02:26.361
&nbsp;When I read scripts today, I’m definitely
looking at that and seeing it add up and I’m

00:02:26.361 --> 00:02:27.890
like okay locations, days.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:33.489
I mean there’s ways you can do some, bringing
consolation and stuff.

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:36.680
It’s not all like, when you’re reading
the full story you’re like “Oh, there’s

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.100
only like four locations here.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.670
Four main locations, it’s easy.”

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:43.060
It makes it look like it moves around a little
bit more, that kind of stuff.&nbsp;

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:50.849
I don’t determine my working on something
in terms of what budget I’m going to do

00:02:50.849 --> 00:02:52.000
it at first right?

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.890
But I’m going to access it from a story
level to see what are the story points that

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:00.390
we need to hit that maybe we don’t have
a chase scene.

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:03.030
Or if we have a chase scene we shoot it a
certain way.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:07.340
So like the chase scene in our movie [A CHILD’S
VOICE] when he goes to pick up the girl and

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:12.500
they shoot, I would have loved to do a whole
different other stuff there but that’s…and

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:24.510
it still worked but I had to do certain angles
just to see the idea and in the editing room

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:29.060
Edgar [Director Edgar Michael Bravo] knew
this but when he was shooting and they had

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:34.469
done the shot list out they figured we can
make this, we can sell this in a day.

00:03:34.469 --> 00:03:40.180
We can do all of that in one day (in actually
a half a day), we shot that entire chase and

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:44.400
rundown scene in a day and it worked.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:49.610
Yes, there’s ways to get around it, you
just have to be creative.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:56.709
Film Courage: Is your goal moving forward
to go past the $250,000 budget or are you

00:03:56.709 --> 00:04:01.569
content staying here knowing that I want to
tell stories that I want to tell, we’re

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:08.329
going to find financing our way and not be
beholden to more money, more problems.

00:04:08.329 --> 00:04:12.599
John: Mo’ Money, Mo’ Problems!

00:04:12.599 --> 00:04:21.019
I mean if there’s something that I’m really
passionate about and I see that we can do

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:24.400
it for X, I’m like Okay, do we want to do
this?

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.110
Do we want to do this without a script supervisor
or a first AD.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:32.099
Edgar does that while he is directing.

00:04:32.099 --> 00:04:38.222
So yes, that’s the hard part but in reality
it’s not like now we’ve graduated to this

00:04:38.222 --> 00:04:40.970
level now I can’t…I never looked at it
that way.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:46.430
I just roll up my sleeves, I do wardrobe,
I do props, I’m doing whatever wherever

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:48.610
there is a hand that’s needed, right?

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:52.741
And it’s not because I love doing it but
it’s needed otherwise I’ve got to come

00:04:52.741 --> 00:04:56.789
up with that cash for somebody else to do
it.&nbsp;

00:04:56.789 --> 00:05:02.490
So in short, yes I would like more money because
I’d like to give the time that we would

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:10.770
love to have where we could do all of the
things that we have to cut out in editing

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.340
because we didn’t have enough time.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:18.729
Or we didn’t get to the actor…we didn’t
have enough time to get the actor to be able

00:05:18.729 --> 00:05:23.729
to get to that level that we wanted and we
only had a certain number of takes that we

00:05:23.729 --> 00:05:27.110
could do because the limitation of budget
and time.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:30.410
These are the things that you have to plan
out and know what you’re doing in order

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:36.080
to execute that and still get a good finished
product but money doesn’t always solve that

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:41.580
if it’s the crux of the issue that is money
then I think you’re going to fail.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:47.060
If it’s innovation and knowing what your
resources are and where you need to put them

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:49.220
(what scenes you need to focus on).

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:53.770
So as a director if you’re focused on getting
Academy Award winning moments every single

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:58.500
take and every single scene, you’re going
to be in deep trouble.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.220
Because in reality you have certain story
points where you definitely need to work with

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:07.220
the actor and you definitely need to build
that up because at the point of where you

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:12.909
are in the story is very important because
you’re coming to the end of the film where

00:06:12.909 --> 00:06:15.800
everybody is riding now.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:20.300
They’ve followed the first and the second
act, now we’re in the home stretch and everything

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.180
has to pay off.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:28.400
So you don’t want to compromise where you’re
like Oh my gosh, I spent all of my time upfront

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:31.680
here front loaded in the story.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.810
Where I really need it to be the most impactful
is in this area where we are seeing the revelations

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:42.360
and all of the turns that are coming, all
the structure that we talked about in the

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.440
screenwriting, all of that has to hit.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:50.580
So you have to make choices because of the
limitations, if you have more money you can

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:53.940
just expand on that, it gives you more breathing
room, it gives you other personnel to come

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:59.840
in and take the concentration of the director
and just focus it solely on the actors, not

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:04.240
have to think about the schedule, not have
to think about where we are in the script,

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:06.780
the continuity, that kind of stuff.&nbsp;

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:12.800
It’s really remarkable, I mean when people
see our films and they go “My God, I can’t

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:18.590
believe you guys did that with such a small
crew, in so few days, with so little money.”

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:23.389
That doesn’t just happen, it’s because
it was planned.

00:07:23.389 --> 00:07:27.129
And I give a lot of that credit to Edgar because
he’s really great with that.

00:07:27.129 --> 00:07:31.470
I mean out of necessity he’s had to do it
but the only reason that I want more money

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:37.639
is that his entire focus could be working
with the actor, getting them to the emotional

00:07:37.639 --> 00:07:42.960
beats that he’s really desiring because
he knows, he gets excited by the potential

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:49.290
of what he’s seeing come out, come through
and working with them and it’s a trust and

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:50.500
it’s such a beautiful thing.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:55.580
I mean as a producer I may never direct a
movie but I can always enjoy the trust between

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:58.139
an actor and a director working together.

00:07:58.139 --> 00:08:05.100
I want to finance that potential and make
sure that that money is put in the appropriate

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.889
places to get the maximum impact so when the
audiences sees it they’re like “Whoah!”

00:08:08.889 --> 00:08:12.099
They’re blown away like that was a great
performance.

00:08:12.099 --> 00:08:13.440
That was real.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.870
That was…I felt I was there.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:17.919
That’s what I’m going for.

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:25.870
So if it comes at $30,000 or $50,000, $100,000
or $200,000 whatever it is (or millions more),

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:28.479
the same result should occur.

00:08:28.479 --> 00:08:33.810
If you do everything correctly, if you follow
what your passions are, you do all those points

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:36.350
that I talked about, I don’t think you can
fail.

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.290
You’re going to learn a lot.

00:08:38.290 --> 00:08:41.080
You may make mistakes but I don’t think
you can fail.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:46.160
You’ll stumble, you’ll stumble forward
to the finish line with a very good product

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:53.380
that can be enjoyed not only through the people
who are going to receive it and see it and

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:54.140
take something from it.

